g the secondary question is the  cost of these MRI scans as they are  quite costly in the US but less so in  Europe now with anything new or novel we  need to keep an open mind because it  challenges our traditional methods of  managing prostate cancer and I want to  spend a few minutes talking about vocals  therapy for many years we've had the  economy of no treatment as you can see  on the left and see that watching it  passively or actively with active  surveillance versus the radical  treatments we have on the right surgery  radiation hoagland brachytherapy cry out  there but the question is can focal  therapy come in and occupy the happy  middle now with focal therapy we have to  utilize a number of avenues to roll this  out safely we have a number of centers  throughout the world that are doing  clinical research on this this is a  systematic review that my group  comprised of an international panel  recently reviewed the literature focal  therapy comes in a variety of fashions I  just wanted to take a moment to explain  them the picture on the top right is  weird all started back in the early  2000s if we found the main  with one side of cancer cancer confined  to one lobe and what you see here is a  picture of the prostate which is right  here and then you have the urethra here  in the center separating right and left  sides and you can see in the red you  have the cancer and each of these yellow  dots on both sides represents the nerve  bundle so in this gentleman he had a  Hemi ablation a cancer on the one lobe  and we ablated the whole side then the  question arises coming oblate perhaps  three quarters of the gland you see in  the bottom left whereby we can almost  eliminate the entire thing and spear the  one nerve and then on the bottom right  as well how far can we really push the  envelope can we do 99% of the gland and  spear that nerve that's a great question  we haven't really been able to achieve  that from a practical point of view but  the way things are going right now is  what you've seen the top left which is  true focal image guided treatment  whereby we're only treating the tumor  with a margin around it to ensure that  we try to get all the cancer now the  flip side of all this is the active  surveillance part as we talked about  vocals a combination of treating the  known cancer and leaving the rest of the  gland alone so back to our model of the  Hemi ablation seen on the top right if  you have blade half the gland by  definition the other half sign after  surveillance three-quarters the bottom  left you voice recorders and you take a  25-percent risk for the other gland but  as you can see with the top left as you  get more targeted with your treatment  you put more of the gland on  surveillance and you take more potential  risk for recurrence so this is a cartoon  depicting the various forms of focal  therapy top left is hope near whole  gland the middle on the top is the hem  ablation the right is the three quarter  and then on the bottom three this is the  newer types of treatment we're talking  about the bottom left is treatment of  two tumors in different parts of the  prostate the bottom middle is what's  called quadrant ablation  and we're purposely treating the nerve  on that side and then the one on the  right is the anterior gland ablation and  you're leaving both nerve bundles so  patient selection has been a work that  people have been working on we've had a  number of consensus panels this is a  systematic review that my international  group has written on bottom line is  there's no consensus as of this  publication back in 2014 for the ideal  candidate earlier most studies presumed  unilateral disease as I mentioned we got  we moved beyond that we at that time  we're using the bigger 50 core mapping  biopsies and we were just beginning to  use MRI however a consensus in 2014 then  showed that we should as dr. Lopez  stated shy away from the low-risk  patient cuz they're better suited for  active surveillance we then focused on  the intermediate risk one the volumes  not a determined since we're only target  in a small area and we want the man to  benefit from this treatment by having a  life expectancy over ten years I wrote  this article with my colleague dr. clots  from Toronto who is a very strong  advocate for active surveillance and we  both agree that there still are low-risk  prostate cancer patients that could  benefit from focal therapy in the area  of active surveillance because the two  approaches indeed can complement one  another so going back to the original  prototype which is the Hemi ablation you  see here we're freezing these are ice  balls we're freezing half of the  prostate here's the ice balls we have  two cancer's located here and I wanted  to go over some of the results based on  that original prototype we can't really  follow the PSA because we know the PSA  drops by about eighty percent but it  doesn't really tell us very much so we  have to rely on biopsy and as you can  see these are small studies but five in  the literature and you look on the  column on the right we see a lesson five  percent failure rate on the treated  I and up to about a 12-percent  positivity on the untreated side which  is basically what we expect anyway in  terms of the complications not many  bowel problems zero the confidence is  upwards of a hundred percent and the  potency in these studies was between  seventeen ninety percent with about half  the men resorting to oral five  phosphodiesterase inhibitors this is a  study reported by douban looking at  focal Hemi ablation again they found in  their series a hundred percent  countenance eighty-six percent potency  and then they tried to do a matched pair  analysis to look for men who failed  focal therapy might need further  treatment and when they compared the  patients on when focal to a cohort  undergoing radical prostatectomy as you  can see here with the p-values it was  not statistically different this is a  study from the University College of  London group using high foo to treat  prostate cancer in a focal manner very  similar results the pad three continents  rate ninety-two percent the potency rate  seventy-seven percent overall this is a  recent report on targeted focal therapy  by david crawford he placed gold seed  targets into the prostate and then  targeted those he found that at one year  eighty percent of the biopsies were  negative and he reported no adverse  events no incontinence nor change in  their sexual function scores so you saw  the prior cartoon of Hemi ablation and  what we're doing now in 2015 and Beyond  is dialing back at our treatment so you  can see here we're not running the ice  all the way to the edge we're now  pushing that ice back further and only  treating a smaller area of the prostate  again this is the patient who has the  Dark Side of the Moon anterior to  and the question is what do we do with  him so you know I'm a surgeon a Duke and  maybe the answer is radical  prostatectomy but these patients they  want more of a guarantee regarding  continents and potency and what can we  offer them so this is a novel concept  this is an anterior zone cryosurgery for  this particular patient as you saw his  tumor was in the anterior zone here  we're running the ice down to here we're  getting nowhere near his nerve bundle on  the left and the right so this patient  has an ideal outcome in terms of potency  and continents so what is the role of  multiparametric MRI in a focal therapy  program again we turn towards a  consensus effort of international  experts comprising radiologists medical  oncologists urologist radiation doctors  and we have these reports we're now  focused on identifying the dominant or  the index lesion we're focused on  intermediate risk cancers for the most  part and MRI can help find these tumors  that are typically larger than 0.5 CCS  and as dr. Gupta has shown you a Gleason  grade of 7 or higher in other words  clinically significant disease [ __ ] I can  localize them characterize them and put  them in 3d space and then we can go back  and treat them the other thing as a  surgeon that I always want to know is  has the cancer escaped so I find it  important to know about extracapsular  extension or ECE this study looked at  patients who underwent surgery so we  know if there was extension outside the  capsule but they had a negative him MRI  and it showed that using the targeted  biopsy scheme that was statistically  significant for predicting extra capture  extension even in the face of a negative  MRI once again supporting the need for  both MRI and targeted boxing combination  you saw this patient earlier this is his  MRI when you look at this you say wow  this is a great candidate for focal  therapy I know where to draw my margins  I can  a quadrant ablation just like this and  that should be quite effective but in  fact as dr. Gupta mentioned we biopsied  this one and it's a high grade and we  try to do the right thing for the  patient so he opted not to go with focal  therapy and went with radical  prostatectomy so MRI can help both ways  and finding candidates and finding men  who are not candidates so what is the  role of MP MRI in focal therapy this is  again an international consensus panel  we should use MRIs the optimal imaging  modality for follow-up after focal  therapy the first one should be done at  six months and then imaging yearly  thereafter if possible so what are some  of the downsides as was mentioned MRI is  challenging it requires expertise and  people who do this all the time we still  need more information on  cost-effectiveness and finally as dr.  Gupta mentioned standardized reporting  is really key and we feel that we're  there now with the pie-rats system so  bringing it all together MP MRI improves  risk stratification for active  surveillance by clinically detecting  significant disease by directing  biopsies to the lesion of suspicion and  it is also integral to focal therapy by  identifying localizing index lesion and  for follow targeted biopsies of that  area we're still working on an  international collaborative group to  push the frontiers of focal therapy our  next meeting is going to be a Duke in  June 2016 this will be our ninth meeting  it's an international working group of  scientists clinicians radiologists  industry medical oncologists etc those  that are trying to make better  treatments for men with prostate cancer  I thank you for your attention  thanks to each of you for your  presentations a quick reminder for our  audience how to submit questions simply  type them in the Q&amp;A box found by  clicking on the green Q&amp;A button at the  lower left of the presentation window  the speakers will answer as many  questions as time permits and will  follow up if we don't have time today  okay so we'll give you a few moments if  there are no questions I would like to  once again thank doctors Loeb Gupta and  pelagic for their presentations and ask  them if they have any final comments to  share with us today Judy thanks so much  um I think that you know one of the  final comments that make is that I think  it's very telling that today's  presentation had two urologists as a as  well as a radiologist and in the  presentation I think it really shows the  importance of the collaborative effort  between radiology neurology to make this  thing a very successful endeavor I'm  very lucky to have folks on my team like  Tom Palaszczuk who will make who support  them or MP MRI and really the role as it  continues to increase  I'd like to thank the audience for  joining us today and just a quick  reminder if you wish to obtain credits  for today's session please click on me  get your free cm ECE credits button  located in the lower left of your screen  this will take you to the continuing  education page where you can select a  speaker click on the CEC any button and  be guided 